{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB14512 - MONDO_6547",
  "verbalization": "Mometasone Furoate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Exanthem (disease) through a chain of indications that link the drug to dermatological conditions, which in turn are associated with the disease. The evidence suggests that Mometasone Furoate is used to treat Contact Dermatitis and Granuloma Annulare, both of which are inflammatory skin conditions. These conditions are linked to Exanthem (disease) via indirect pathways, as the drug's use in these indications may influence immune responses or inflammatory processes that contribute to the development of the disease. The presence of multiple indirect pathways, including the use of related compounds like Prednicarbate and Desoximetasone, highlights a potential role of corticosteroids in modulating inflammatory responses, which could be a mechanism by which Mometasone Furoate contributes to the manifestation of Exanthem. While the exact mechanism remains unclear, the integration of these pathways suggests a possible link between corticosteroid use and the development of Exanthem, emphasizing the importance of considering the broader context of drug indications in understanding its therapeutic effects.",
